  "body": {
    "id": "thm:polynomial-oracle-frontier",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Suppose \\(C>0\\) and the sequential multiscale certificate holds without a dyadic loss, with \\(T_\\beta\\) large enough that \\(L_T\\le T/4\\). Then, for every \\(T\\ge T_\\beta\\), \\[\\frac{\\kappa_\\beta}{2}\\min\\{B^2,(B\\wedge C)^2\\}T^{-2\\beta/(2\\beta+1)}\\le R_T^{(2)}(\\beta;B,C)\\le K_+(B,C,\\beta)T^{-2\\beta/(2\\beta+1)}.\\] Thus \\(R_T^{(2)}(\\beta;B,C)=\\Theta_{B,C,\\beta}(T^{-2\\beta/(2\\beta+1)})\\) over all sequential policies and all estimators.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "C",
      "T",
      "L_T",
      "T_\\beta",
      "\\kappa_\\beta",
      "\\beta",
      "B",
      "R_T^{(2)}(\\beta;B,C)",
      "K_+(B,C,\\beta)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:minimax-mse",
      "def:oracle-scale",
      "thm:oracle-upper",
      "oeq:sequential-multiscale-certificate"
    ],
    "justification": "This downstream theorem assembles the inherited upper audit and the novel certificate into the exact price of polynomial memory over the strongest sequential design comparison class.",
    "gap": "The matched frontier is the direct payoff from the certificate rather than a second root contribution. Bojinov, Simchi-Levi, and Zhao treat finite-order regular designs, while Hu and Wager and Jia, Kallus, and Yu impose geometric stochastic mixing.",
    "consumer": "The result certifies that the power-law operational window is information-theoretically necessary, rather than an artifact of one estimator.",
    "proof_tex": "Assume the sequential multiscale certificate in the statement, and put \\(D=B\\wedge C\\) and \\(x=T^{1/(2\\beta+1)}\\).  Since the assumed threshold ensures \\(L_T\\le T/4\\), the certificate applies at \\(L=L_T\\).  For every fixed \\(\\pi\\in\\mathcal D_T\\), it supplies before assignment a prior \\(Q_{\\pi,L_T}\\) supported on \\(\\mathcal P_{T,\\beta}(B,C)\\).  Therefore, for every estimator \\(\\widehat\\theta\\),\n\\[\n \\sup_{Y\\in\\mathcal P_{T,\\beta}(B,C)}\n \\mathbb E_{\\pi,Y}[\\{\\widehat\\theta-\\theta_T(Y)\\}^2]\n \\ge\n \\mathbb E_{Q_{\\pi,L_T},\\pi}\n [\\{\\widehat\\theta-\\theta_T(Y)\\}^2].\n\\]\nTaking the infimum over estimators and invoking the stipulated certificate yields\n\\[\n \\inf_{\\widehat\\theta}\\sup_Y\n \\mathbb E_{\\pi,Y}[\\{\\widehat\\theta-\\theta_T(Y)\\}^2]\n \\ge\\kappa_\\beta\\min\\left\\{\\frac{B^2L_T}{T},D^2L_T^{-2\\beta}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(x\\ge1\\), \\(\\max\\{1,\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\}\\ge x/2\\) and \\(\\max\\{1,\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\}\\le x\\).  Hence\n\\[\n \\frac{L_T}{T}\\ge\\frac12T^{-2\\beta/(2\\beta+1)},\n \\qquad\n L_T^{-2\\beta}\\ge T^{-2\\beta/(2\\beta+1)}.\n\\]\nIt follows that\n\\[\n \\min\\left\\{\\frac{B^2L_T}{T},D^2L_T^{-2\\beta}\\right\\}\n \\ge\\frac12\\min\\{B^2,D^2\\}T^{-2\\beta/(2\\beta+1)}.\n\\]\nThis bound holds for every \\(\\pi\\), so taking the design infimum in \\(\\mathrm{def{:}minimax\\mbox{-}mse}\\) proves the lower inequality.  The upper inequality is exactly \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}oracle\\mbox{-}upper}\\).  Since \\(B>0\\), \\(C>0\\), and \\(\\kappa_\\beta>0\\), the lower constant is positive, while the displayed definition of \\(K_+\\) is finite.  The two inequalities therefore imply the stated \\(\\Theta_{B,C,\\beta}\\) conclusion.",
